<body>
<div class="container">

<div class="header">
  <h1>Amazon Bestsellers: Price vs. User Rating Trend Analysis</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">A decade-long examination (2009–2019) of how pricing dynamics relate to customer satisfaction across Fiction and Non-Fiction bestsellers — built to inform pricing, curation, and genre investment decisions.</p>
  <div class="meta">
    <span><strong>Dataset</strong> 550 annual top-50 entries</span>
    <span><strong>Window</strong> 11 years (2009–2019)</span>
    <span><strong>Audience</strong> Management / Strategy</span>
    <span><strong>Prepared</strong> April 2026</span>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="panel">
  <h2><span class="eyebrow">Executive Summary</span>Key findings at a glance</h2>
  <p class="lead">Across 550 annual top-50 bestseller entries spanning 2009–2019, the relationship between price and customer rating is <strong>weakly negative</strong> — higher-priced books trend toward <em>lower</em> ratings, not higher. Over the decade, average bestseller prices <strong>declined by roughly 35%</strong> while average ratings <strong>rose steadily</strong>, indicating a market shift toward affordable, high-satisfaction titles.</p>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Total Records</div><div class="val">550</div><div class="sub">351 unique titles · 248 authors</div></div>
    <div class="kpi alt"><div class="label">Avg. Book Price</div><div class="val">$13.10</div><div class="sub">Median $11 · Range $0–$105</div></div>
    <div class="kpi good"><div class="label">Avg. User Rating</div><div class="val">4.62</div><div class="sub">Range 3.3 – 4.9 (of 5.0)</div></div>
    <div class="kpi warn"><div class="label">Price ↔ Rating r</div><div class="val">−0.133</div><div class="sub">Pearson · p ≈ 0.002 (significant)</div></div>
    <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Avg. Reviews</div><div class="val">11,953</div><div class="sub">Max &gt; 87,000</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout key">
    <strong>The headline:</strong> Paying more does not buy better reviews. The cheapest segment ($0–$5) has the <em>highest</em> average rating (4.72); the $16–$20 segment has the <em>lowest</em> (4.50). Fiction is cheaper on average ($10.85 vs. $14.84) yet rated higher (4.65 vs. 4.60) and generates <strong>73% more reviews per title</strong> than Non-Fiction.
  </div>

  <h3>Table of Contents</h3>
  <div class="toc">
    <a href="#market-overview">1 · Bestseller Market Overview</a>
    <a href="#price-rating-correlation">2 · Price vs. Rating Correlation</a>
    <a href="#genre-comparison">3 · Fiction vs. Non-Fiction</a>
    <a href="#temporal-trends">4 · Year-over-Year Trends</a>
    <a href="#top-authors">5 · Dominant Authors</a>
    <a href="#recommendations">6 · Strategic Recommendations</a>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<div class="panel" id="market-overview">
  <h2><span class="eyebrow">Section 1</span>Bestseller Market Overview (2009–2019)</h2>
  <p>The dataset represents Amazon's top-50 bestselling books for each year from 2009 through 2019 — 550 annual listings covering <strong>351 unique titles</strong> from <strong>248 distinct authors</strong>. Non-Fiction slightly outnumbers Fiction (56.4% vs. 43.6%) across the decade.</p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="chart-wrap"><h4>Genre Share of Bestseller Listings</h4><div class="chart-box"><canvas id="genreDonut"></canvas></div></div>
    <div class="chart-wrap"><h4>Listings per Year (Fiction vs. Non-Fiction)</h4><div class="chart-box"><canvas id="listingsYear"></canvas></div></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Decade-level summary statistics</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th class="num">Overall</th><th class="num">Fiction</th><th class="num">Non-Fiction</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Records</td><td class="num">550</td><td class="num">240</td><td class="num">310</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Average price ($)</td><td class="num">13.10</td><td class="num">10.85</td><td class="num">14.84</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Median price ($)</td><td class="num">11.00</td><td class="num">9.00</td><td class="num">12.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Average user rating</td><td class="num">4.618</td><td class="num">4.648</td><td class="num">4.595</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Average reviews</td><td class="num">11,953</td><td class="num">15,684</td><td class="num">9,065</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Observation:</strong> Non-Fiction carries higher price points (mean +$4 and median +$3) but commands lower average engagement (reviews) and slightly lower ratings than Fiction. Fiction is the cheaper, more satisfying, more-reviewed category on the bestseller list.</div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<div class="panel" id="price-rating-correlation">
  <h2><span class="eyebrow">Section 2</span>Price vs. User Rating: Correlation Analysis</h2>
  <p>To answer the central question — does price drive satisfaction? — we compute the <strong>Pearson correlation coefficient</strong> between Price and User Rating across all 550 records.</p>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi warn"><div class="label">Pearson r (All)</div><div class="val">−0.133</div><div class="sub">t = −3.14 · p ≈ 0.0017</div></div>
    <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Fiction r</div><div class="val">−0.103</div><div class="sub">p ≈ 0.110 (not significant)</div></div>
    <div class="kpi alt"><div class="label">Non-Fiction r</div><div class="val">−0.135</div><div class="sub">p ≈ 0.017 (significant)</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-wrap"><h4>Price vs. User Rating — All Bestsellers (with linear fit)</h4><div class="chart-box tall"><canvas id="scatter"></canvas></div></div>

  <h3>Average rating by price segment</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Price band</th><th class="num">Titles</th><th class="num">Avg. rating</th><th class="num">Avg. reviews</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>$0 – $5</td><td class="num">87</td><td class="num"><span class="ind-up">4.718</span></td><td class="num">10,285</td></tr>
      <tr><td>$6 – $10</td><td class="num">179</td><td class="num">4.649</td><td class="num">13,452</td></tr>
      <tr><td>$11 – $15</td><td class="num">142</td><td class="num">4.578</td><td class="num">14,605</td></tr>
      <tr><td>$16 – $20</td><td class="num">74</td><td class="num"><span class="ind-dn">4.499</span></td><td class="num">9,447</td></tr>
      <tr><td>$21 – $30</td><td class="num">42</td><td class="num">4.676</td><td class="num">7,401</td></tr>
      <tr><td>$30+</td><td class="num">26</td><td class="num">4.538</td><td class="num">7,220</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout key">
    <strong>Interpretation:</strong> The correlation is <em>statistically significant but weak</em> — price explains less than 2% of rating variance (r² ≈ 0.018). The direction is unambiguous, however: as price rises, ratings drift down. The segment plot makes this even clearer — the sweet spot for customer satisfaction sits at the <strong>$0–$10 range</strong>, while the "premium paperback/hardcover" $16–$20 band shows the lowest satisfaction of any common segment.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<div class="panel" id="genre-comparison">
  <h2><span class="eyebrow">Section 3</span>Fiction vs. Non-Fiction: Pricing &amp; Rating Strategies</h2>
  <p>Fiction and Non-Fiction behave like two structurally different markets on the bestseller list. The table below makes the differences scannable.</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th class="num">Fiction</th><th class="num">Non-Fiction</th><th class="num">Gap</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Share of listings</td><td class="num">43.6%</td><td class="num">56.4%</td><td class="num">NF +12.8 pp</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Average price</td><td class="num">$10.85</td><td class="num">$14.84</td><td class="num"><span class="ind-dn">NF +$3.99</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Median price</td><td class="num">$9</td><td class="num">$12</td><td class="num">NF +$3</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Average user rating</td><td class="num">4.648</td><td class="num">4.595</td><td class="num"><span class="ind-up">Fic +0.053</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Average reviews per book</td><td class="num">15,684</td><td class="num">9,065</td><td class="num"><span class="ind-up">Fic +73%</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pearson r (Price vs. Rating)</td><td class="num">−0.103</td><td class="num">−0.135</td><td class="num">Both negative</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="chart-wrap"><h4>Average Price by Genre (per year)</h4><div class="chart-box"><canvas id="genrePrice"></canvas></div></div>
    <div class="chart-wrap"><h4>Average Rating by Genre (per year)</h4><div class="chart-box"><canvas id="genreRating"></canvas></div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Value verdict:</strong> Fiction delivers better perceived value on every dimension except catalog depth. Non-Fiction is priced as if it's a premium good, yet receives lower ratings and fewer reviews per title. The premium is not earning its keep on the satisfaction side of the ledger.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<div class="panel" id="temporal-trends">
  <h2><span class="eyebrow">Section 4 · Most critical</span>Year-over-Year Trends in Pricing and Ratings</h2>
  <p>The most strategically important view in this report: how pricing and ratings have <em>moved</em> over the decade. Two opposing trends dominate — prices fell materially while ratings climbed — with several identifiable inflection years.</p>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi"><div class="label">Avg. Price 2009</div><div class="val">$15.40</div><div class="sub">Start of window</div></div>
    <div class="kpi alt"><div class="label">Avg. Price 2019</div><div class="val">$10.08</div><div class="sub"><span class="ind-dn">−34.5% vs. 2009</span></div></div>
    <div class="kpi good"><div class="label">Avg. Rating 2009</div><div class="val">4.58</div><div class="sub">Start of window</div></div>
    <div class="kpi good"><div class="label">Avg. Rating 2019</div><div class="val">4.74</div><div class="sub"><span class="ind-up">+0.16 pts vs. 2009</span></div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-wrap"><h4>Average Bestseller Price, 2009–2019 (overall + by genre)</h4><div class="chart-box tall"><canvas id="priceTrend"></canvas></div></div>
  <div class="chart-wrap"><h4>Average User Rating, 2009–2019 (overall + by genre)</h4><div class="chart-box tall"><canvas id="ratingTrend"></canvas></div></div>
  <div class="chart-wrap"><h4>Average Reviews per Title, 2009–2019</h4><div class="chart-box"><canvas id="reviewTrend"></canvas></div></div>

  <h3>Annual metrics table</h3>
  <table>
